          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            is called
            %em sequentially compact
            if every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequence"} sequence
            of points in that space has a sub-sequence which
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convergence"} converges
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+space"} compactness
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+spaces"} metric spaces
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+space"} Compactness
            is an extremely useful concept in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+space"} compact
            if it isn’t “fuzzy around the edges”.
          %p
            Whilst one can study a topological space by itself, it is often useful to probe it with known spaces. A common choice for topological spaces, and in particular metric spaces, is to use the natural numbers
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/one-point+compactification"} one-point compactification
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo ∪
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N} \cup \{*\}
            of the natural numbers. This is more traditionally known as studying the topology using sequences and convergent sequences.
          %p
            Thus one can ask, “Can I detect compactness using probes from
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            = succeed "?\u201D." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mo ∪
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N} \cup \{*\}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            and
            = succeed "?\u201D." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mo ∪
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N} \cup \{*\}
          %p
            Thus
            %strong sequential compactness
            is what
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+space"} compactness
            looks like if all one has to test it are sequences.
          %h2#definition Definition
          #seqcpt.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A topological space is
              %strong sequentially compact
              if every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequence"} sequence
              in it has a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convergence"} convergent
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsequence"} subsequence
          %h2#properties Properties
          %p The following is a list of properties of and pertaining to sequentially compact spaces.
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                For a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+space"} metric space
                = succeed "." do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequentially+compact+metric+spaces+are+equivalently+compact+metric+spaces"} sequentially compact metric spaces are equivalently compact metric spaces
            %li
              %p
                The
                %span.newWikiWord
                  Eberlein–Šmulian theorem
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Eberlein-Smulian+theorem"} ?
                states that in a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Banach+space"} Banach space
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+topology"} weak topology
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countably+compact+space"} countable compactness
            %li
              %p
                A
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable+set"} countable
                product of sequentially compact spaces is again sequentially compact.
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{X_j\}
              be a countable family of sequentially compact spaces. Let
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mi k
                    %mo :
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mi j
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi j
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_k: \prod_j X_j \to X_k
            %p
              denote the
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi k
                      %mi th
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k^{th}
              projection, and let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x(n))
              be a sequence in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mi j
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prod X_j
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi j
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_j = x_j(n)
              as follows. For
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi j
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j = 1
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_1
              of the given sequence
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x_n)
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1(1) = x(1)
              and such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
