                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi x
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi n
                              %mi j
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_{n_j}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                say, and by continuity
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi y
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi n
                            %mi j
                      %mo =
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi n
                            %mi j
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y_{n_j} = f(x_{n_j})
                converges to
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x)
          %h3#relationship_to_compactness Relationship to Compactness
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+space"} Compactness
            does not imply sequentially compactness, nor does sequentially compactness imply compactness, without further assumptions, see at
            %em
              %a{:href => "#Examples"} Examples and counter-examples
            below.
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+spaces"} metric spaces
            for example both notions coincide, see at
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequentially+compact+metric+spaces+are+equivalently+compact+metric+spaces"} sequentially compact metric spaces are equivalently compact metric spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lebesgue+number+lemma"} Lebesgue number lemma
            and the fact that
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequentially+compact+metric+spaces+are+totally+bounded"} sequentially compact metric spaces are totally bounded
          %p
            This is
            %em not
            a contradiction to the statement that compact is equivalent to every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/net"} net
            having a convergent subnet: Given a sequence in a compact space, its convergent
            %em subnet
            need not be a
            %em subsequence
            (see
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/net"} net
            for a definition of subnet).
          %h2#Examples Examples and counter-examples
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+space"} metric space
            is sequentially compact precisely if it is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+topological+space"} compact
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequentially+compact+metric+spaces+are+equivalently+compact+metric+spaces"} sequentially compact metric spaces are equivalently compact metric spaces
          %p In general neither of these two properties implies the other:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Examples of a sequentially compact spaces which are not compact are given in in (
                %a{:href => "#BuskesRooij97"}> Buskes-Rooij 97, example 13.5
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "#Patty08"} Patty 08, chapter 4, example 13
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "#Vermeeren10"} Vermeeren 10, prop. 18
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/long+line"} long line
                is such an example.
            %li
              %p
                An example of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+topological+space"} compact topological space
                which is not sequentially compact is given in (
                %a{:href => "#SteenSeebach70"}> Steen-Seebach 70, item 105
                ), recalled at
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "#Vermeeren10"} Vermeeren 10, prop. 17
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "compact+space#CompactSpacesWhichAreNotSequentiallyCompact"} compact space – Compact spaces which are not sequentially compact
          #ACompactSpaceNotSequentiallyCompact.num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p
              %strong (a compact space which is not sequentially compact)
            %p
              Consider the following uncountable power of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+space"} discrete space
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+topological+space"} product topological space
              (with its
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tychonoff+topology"} Tychonoff topology
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ≔
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo :
                        %mi ℕ
                        %mo →
                        %mn 2
                    %mi Disc
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    X \coloneqq \underset{f: \mathbb{N} \to 2}{\prod} Disc(\{0,1\})
            %p
              of copies of the discrete space on two elements, indexed by functions
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mo →
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: \mathbb{N} \to \{0, 1\}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Disc
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Disc(\{0,1\})
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+topological+space"} finite
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+topological+space"} discrete topological space
              it is clearly compact. Therefore the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tychonoff+theorem"} Tychonoff theorem
              says that also
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is compact.
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi n
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo :
                        %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x_n)_{n: \mathbb{N}}
              be the sequence in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              given by the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality"} double-dual embedding
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mn 2
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mn 2
                          %mi ℕ
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N} \to 2^{2^\mathbb{N}},
            %p
              i.e., define
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_n
              to have coordinate at
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mo →
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: \mathbb{N} \to 2
              given by
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi n
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi f
                      %mo =
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x_n)_f = f(n)
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is not sequentially compact. We will argue by contradiction. (This
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/refutation+by+contradiction"} refutation by contradiction
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+logic"} constructively valid
            %p
              Suppose instead some subsequence
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi n
                          %mi k
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x_{(n_k)})_{k \in \mathbb{N}}
              converges to some
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
